SAS provides several ways to output PDF files, including the `ods pdf` statement. To create a PDF with a journal format, you'll need to use a combination of `ods pdf` and some formatting options.

Here's an example code snippet that demonstrates how to output a PDF in a journal format:
```sas
ods pdf file="output.pdf" startpage=node;
ods layout gridded columns=2 gap=0.1in;
ods region borderwidth=1pt;
ods table SpreadSheetColHeader / style(header) = [font_face="Arial" font_size=10pt]
                           style(cell) = [font_face="Arial" font_size=8pt]
                           style(footer) = [font_face="Arial" font_size=8pt];

/* Your SAS code to produce the journal-style output goes here */
data journal;
  set sashelp.cars;
  format make $20.; /* adjust the length of the MAKE variable to match your needs */
  put make /;
  put _all_;
run;
/* End of journal-style output code */

ods layout end;
ods pdf close;
```
Here's a brief explanation of the code:

* `ods pdf file="output.pdf" startpage=node;` specifies the output PDF file and starts a new page.
* `ods layout gridded columns=2 gap=0.1in;` sets up a grid layout with two columns and a 0.1-inch gap between the columns.
* `ods region borderwidth=1pt;` draws a 1-point border around the region.
* `ods table SpreadSheetColHeader / ...;` specifies the style for the column headers, cell content, and footer. You can adjust the font, size, and other formatting options to match your journal format requirements.

In the example code, we create a simple data set `journal` and use the `put` statement to produce the journal-style output. You'll need to replace this section with your actual SAS code that produces the data you want to include in the PDF.

Finally, `ods layout end;` ends the layout, and `ods pdf close;` closes the PDF output.

When you run this code, SAS will generate a PDF file named "output.pdf" with a journal-style format that matches the settings specified in the `ods table` statement.

